.TH SHARDPOSTCTL-SIEVE 1 "2026-10-01" "ShardPost 0.35.2" "ShardPost Manual"
.SH NAME
shardpostctl-sieve \- manage Sieve scripts and test them without side effects
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shardpostctl sieve list
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I account
.br
.B shardpostctl sieve show
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I account name
.br
.B shardpostctl sieve put
.RB [ \-activate ]
.RB [ \-create ]
.RB [ \-if-match
.IR etag ]
.RB [ \-file
.IR path ]
.I account name
.br
.B shardpostctl sieve delete
.RB [ \-if-match
.IR etag ]
.I account name
.br
.B shardpostctl sieve activate
.I account name
.br
.B shardpostctl sieve deactivate
.I account
.br
.B shardpostctl sieve check
.RB [ \-name
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-file
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-message
.IR mailbox:uidvalidity:uid ]
.RB [ \-from
.IR address ]
.RB [ \-to
.IR address ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I account
.SH DESCRIPTION
The account's Sieve scripts, the same ones ManageSieve serves. Exactly
one script can be active; it is the one that runs on delivery.
.PP
The script
.B jmap-vacation
belongs to the server: it is a copy of the account's JMAP
VacationResponse, written whenever a JMAP client changes that setting,
so that the setting can be seen. Delivery does not read it; it answers
from the setting itself, after the active script, when the active script
sent no vacation reply of its own. The script is listed, can be shown
and checked, and is never the active script;
.BR put ,
.B delete
and
.B activate
refuse it.
.SS sieve list
Every script with its size, its version (ETag) and a marker on the active
one. Names and sizes only, no content, so the role
.B diagnose
is enough.
.SS sieve show
The script text, with its ETag. Reads content, so it needs the role
.BR content .
.SS sieve put
Stores a script from
.B \-file
or standard input. The server compiles it first, with the account's own
includes; a script that does not compile is refused and nothing is
stored.
.B \-activate
makes it the active script in the same step.
.B \-create
stores it only if no script of that name exists yet, and
.B \-if-match
stores it only if the stored script is still the version you read.
Role:
.BR support .
.SS sieve delete
Deletes a script. The active script cannot be deleted; deactivate it or
activate another one first. Role:
.BR support .
.SS sieve activate, sieve deactivate
Selects the active script, or switches filtering off entirely.
.B deactivate
takes no script name. Role:
.BR support .
.SS sieve check
Compiles a script and, if you give it a message, runs it and reports what
it would do.
.PP
Nothing is carried out. No mail is redirected, no vacation reply is sent,
no notification goes out, no external program runs (an
.I execute
or
.I filter
test is treated as if the program had exited 0), no duplicate or vacation
record is written, and the message is not filed anywhere. Every
suppressed side effect is listed under
.IR suppressed ,
so you can see exactly what would have happened in production.
.PP
The message is checked the way a delivery sees it: with the
.B Return-Path
and
.B Delivered-To
fields a delivery adds on top. A vacation reply is reported with its
effective period, after the vacation period settings
.RI ( sieve_vacation_min_period ", " sieve_vacation_max_period ", " sieve_vacation_default_period
in
.IR sieve.settings ):
in the JSON output as
.I days
and
.IR seconds ,
in the text output as "every N days", "every N seconds" or "for every
message" (a period of 0).
.PP
When the server runs global scripts around the account's own
.RI ( sieve.before_dir ", " sieve.after_dir ),
the check runs the same sequence a delivery runs, with the checked script
in the place of the active one, and lists the scripts that ran under
.IR scripts .
A script that does not keep the message ends the sequence. A global
script that fails is skipped, as in a delivery, and is reported as a
failed script. An account without an active script can then be checked
too, against a stored message: give
.B \-message
and neither
.B \-name
nor
.BR \-file .
.PP
Which script is checked:
.B \-file
or standard input for a script you are working on,
.B \-name
for a stored one, and with neither, the active script.
.PP
Which message it runs on:
.B \-message
names a stored message as
.IR mailbox:uidvalidity:uid ,
or standard input carries a test message when no script is read from
there.
.B \-from
and
.B \-to
set the envelope; without
.BR \-to ,
the account is the recipient. Without a message the script is only
compiled.
.PP
Role:
.B content
when the script or the message comes from the store, otherwise
.BR diagnose .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-file " path"
Read the script (or the test message) from this file instead of standard
input.
.TP
.B \-activate
Activate the script that is being stored.
.TP
.B \-create
Refuse to overwrite an existing script.
.TP
.BI \-if-match " etag"
Store or delete only if the stored script is still this version
(exit code 65 otherwise).
.TP
.BI \-name " name"
Check this stored script.
.TP
.BI \-message " mailbox:uidvalidity:uid"
Run the check on this stored message.
.TP
.BI \-from " address"
.TQ
.BI \-to " address"
Envelope sender and recipient of the check.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the answer as JSON.
.SH EXAMPLES
What does the account filter with, and how does it look?
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl sieve list user@example.org
shardpostctl sieve show user@example.org main
.RE
.fi
.PP
Install a script from a file and switch it on:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl sieve put -activate user@example.org main < main.sieve
.RE
.fi
.PP
Before you change the live script, try the new one against a message the
user complains about. Nothing is delivered, forwarded or answered:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl sieve check -file new.sieve \\
    -message INBOX:1789726963:933 user@example.org
.RE
.fi
.PP
"Why did this mail end up in Junk?" Run the active script over it:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl sieve check -message INBOX:1789726963:933 user@example.org
.RE
.fi
.PP
Check a script against a message you have as a file, without touching the
account's data:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl sieve check -file rules.sieve \\
    -from sender@example.net user@example.org < sample.eml
.RE
.fi
.PP
Replace a script safely, so a parallel change is not lost:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl sieve show user@example.org main      # prints the etag
shardpostctl sieve put -if-match '"s-1f0c3a9d2b4e5f60"' \\
    user@example.org main < main.sieve
.RE
.fi
.PP
Switch filtering off for a while, then back on:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl sieve deactivate user@example.org
shardpostctl sieve activate user@example.org main
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Done; for
.B sieve check
the script compiles.
.TP
.B 64
The script does not compile
.RB ( "sieve put" );
nothing was stored, and the message says where the error is.
.TP
.B 65
The checked script does not compile
.RB ( "sieve check" ),
the ETag no longer matches, the script is active and cannot be
deleted, or the script is
.B jmap-vacation
and cannot be changed here.
.TP
.B 67
No such account, script or message.
.TP
.B 77
The caller lacks the role.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR shardpostctl (1),
.BR shardpostctl-learning (1),
.BR shardpostctl-message (1)
.PP
IMAPSieve and the learning rules are described in
.IR docs/imapsieve.md .
